tify abnormal or harmful changes (mutations) in genes, situating it as the mechanism for detecting and characterizing mutations in medical contexts [Data: Entities (3314); Relationships (6533)].\\n\\nThe relationship between GENETIC TEST and GENES formalizes this purpose: genetic tests are used to find abnormal changes (mutations) in genes. This anchors the testing concept to a target substrate (genes) and a target signal (mutations) [Data: Relationships (6533); Entities (3314)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Somatic/acquired mutations are emphasized as non-inherited changes occurring later in life and in only some cells\",\"explanation\":\"ACQUIRED MUTATION is described as a somatic mutation that occurs spontaneously later in life or due to environmental factors and is present in only some cells rather than every cell, distinguishing it from inherited changes [Data: Entities (3496)].\\n\\nSOMATIC MUTATION is similarly characterized as a non-inherited genetic change that arises during life (often from errors during cell division) and is found only in a subset of cells; it can be confined to particular tissues or cell populations such as those within a tumor [Data: Entities (3316)].\\n\\nThe patient-level relationship reinforces timing and distribution: mutations can occur later in life in some of the patient’s cells, connecting the acquired/somatic concept to an individual’s cellular mosaic rather than a whole-body inherited state [Data: Relationships (6990); Entities (3496)].\"},{\"summary\":\"The community distinguishes tumor-focused testing (tumor testing) as a basic type of genetic testing used in care\",\"explanation\":\"TUMOR TESTING is presented as a genetic testing approach that analyzes tumor-related genetic changes to inform management, highlighting a practical pathway for applying genetic insights to clinical decisions [Data: Entities (3484)].\\n\\nGENETIC TEST is explicitly linked to TUMOR TESTING as “another basic type of genetic testing used in prostate cancer care,” framing tumor testing as a subset or modality within the broader genetic testing landscape [Data: Relationships (7020); Entities (3314, 3484)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Somatic mutation concepts are operationally connected to genetic testing because somatic changes may be restricted to tumor cells\",\"explanation\":\"The network explicitly ties SOMATIC MUTATION to GENETIC TEST by noting that genetic testing is described as finding mutations and distinguishing somatic mutations as those present only in tumor cells. This establishes why tumor-oriented genetic testing is necessary: certain clinically relevant mutations may be absent from non-tumor tissue and therefore require tumor-focused sampling/testing strategies [Data: Relationships (7135); Entities (3314, 3316)].\\n\\nThis linkage also integrates the conceptual distinction (somatic vs inherited) into testing choices: when mutations are confined to tumor cells, a testing approach that evaluates tumor tissue (i.e., tumor testing) becomes directly relevant to identifying actionable genetic changes [Data: Entities (3316, 3484); Relationships (7020, 7135)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Biomarker testing is positioned as a detection method for mutation presence/absence, supporting treatment-relevant characterization\",\"explanation\":\"The relationship between MUTATION and BIOMARKER TESTING specifies that biomarker testing can identify the presence or absence of mutations, positioning biomarker testing as a key detection layer in the clinical pipeline for determining mutation status [Data: Relationships (2531); Entities (1553)].\\n\\nSeparately, MUTATION is described as a biomarker-relevant error or change in genetic code and explicitly notes that biomarker testing can identify mutations relevant to cancer treatment choices, indicating the downstream decision relevance of detecting mutation status (even though the specific treatments are not enumerated in the provided data) [Data: Entities (1553); Relationships (2531, 3031)].\"},{\"